<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert Suerbeer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200–<a href="/years/1273">1273</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert Suerbeer was a German churchman who became the first archbishop of Riga. Earlier archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a> in Ireland and papal legate for the Baltic lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, he took up the see of Riga, raised to an archbishopric in 1255, with authority over the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. His rule was marked by disputes with the Livonian Order and the city of Riga.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-suerbeer.jpg" alt="Albert Suerbeer" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SVG created by the uploader · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archbishopric_of_Riga.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Albert Suerbeer was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he took the degree of magister, and became a canon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Albert of Riga</a> in 1229, Archbishop Gerhard of Oldenburg of Bremen appointed him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Riga, but the canons of Riga refused to recognise him and elected their own candidate, Nicholas, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> confirmed in 1231.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1240 Suerbeer became archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a> and primate of Ireland, where he was known as Alberic the German. After the First Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in 1245 he left Ireland, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> needed him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in his struggle with Emperor Frederick II. The pope named him archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and later legate to Gotland, Holstein, Rügen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and in 1246 also gave him the vacant diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>. A determined champion of papal power, he aimed to bring the whole eastern Baltic under the Holy See, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, wary of him, warned him to stay out of Prussia.</p><p>When Bishop Nicholas of Riga died in 1253, Suerbeer at last received the see he had claimed for more than ten years. Under a compromise arranged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-modena">William of Modena</a> he promised to stop working against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>. His suffragan sees included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Dorpat</a>, Ösel-Wiek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>, Sambia, Pomesania, Warmia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Culmerland</a>. His missions to the pagans and his plans for a church union with the Russian principalities brought fresh conflict with the Order, which, with its presence across many lands and its greater wealth, won papal support more easily.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1267 Suerbeer allied himself with Gunzelin, a son of Count Gunzelin III of Schwerin who had come to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> as a crusader, and made him advocate, or governor, of his diocese. While Gunzelin was raising troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the Livonian Order arrested the archbishop and held him on bread and water until he recognised its authority. He died in Riga in 1273, his efforts to win the Baltic for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> having yielded little.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albert Suerbeer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1255 Riga was raised to an archbishopric under <b>Albert Suerbeer</b>, with authority over the bishoprics of Livonia and Prussia.</blockquote><a href="/state/archbishopric-of-riga">Archbishopric of Riga</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Riga, in what is now <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.94,6.96&amp;year=1247" aria-label="Cologne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Albert Suerbeer"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5374 5752h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5374 5752h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.94,6.96&amp;year=1247">What was here in 1247? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
